.TH IRQ_MSI 2
.SH NAME
irq_msi \- a message-signalled interrupt for a driver (SYS_IRQ_MSI)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long irq_msi(long port, uint32_t msg[2]);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B irq_msi
takes a free message-signalled interrupt (PCI MSI or MSI-X) for the
caller's device and binds it to
.IR port ,
one of the caller's ports, on the next processor in turn. It is delivered
as a line bound with
.BR irq_bind (2)
is, and answered with
.BR irq_ack (2).
.PP
.I msg
receives the address
.RI ( msg[0] )
and data
.RI ( msg[1] )
the driver writes into the device's MSI capability or MSI-X table
(user/lib/pci.c does it). The interrupt is edge-triggered: the device
sends one message per event. It is free again when the port goes.
.PP
x86-64 only, 16 of them for the whole system. Drivers only.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The interrupt number, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
The caller is not a driver.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I port
is not the caller's.
.TP
.B EBUSY
All of them are taken.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I msg
is not writable.
.TP
.B ENOSYS
Not on this architecture (arm64).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR irq_bind (2),
.BR ipc_recv (2),
.BR pci_read (2)
